Hi @pcbeingused333, thank you very much for the PR!

Just one suggestion: would it make sense to do the reverse and keep auto as the default, falling back to the explicit classical TLS groups only when the runtime does not support PQC?

I think this would be more future-proof. It would allow to use the groups supported rather than us maintaining a fixed list, including current and future post-quantum tls key exchange groups such as: X25519MLKEM768, SecP256r1MLKEM768, SecP384r1MLKEM1024

Something along these lines?
```javascript
const tls = require('node:tls');

try {
  // Prefer the runtime defaults so supported classical, hybrid,
  // and future groups can be negotiated automatically.
  tls.createSecureContext({ ecdhCurve: 'auto' });
} catch {
  // Compatibility fallback for older Node/OpenSSL versions.
  tls.DEFAULT_ECDH_CURVE =
    'X25519:P-256:X448:P-521:P-384:ffdhe2048:ffdhe3072:ffdhe4096:ffdhe6144:ffdhe8192';
}
```
